This course offers an introduction to the poetry (and prose) of the Romantic period with close attention to the literary texts. Major poets to be studied include Blake, Wordsworth, Coleridge, Byron, Shelley, Keats; works in prose include essays by Lamb and De Quincey. Emphasis will be placed on the complex literary and philosophical questions raised by Romantic writers and the influence of post-Enlightenment thought on modern life. 

Students considering this course should be prepared for: 
1) poetry (lyric, narrative, dramatic) as the dominant genre of this period;
2) a certain degree of conceptual difficulty in textual interpretation;
3) weekly group discussion and collaboration in a creative project;

Class is conducted in English.

課程目標:
The principal aims of this course are to:
(i) develop an understanding of the thermodynamic basis of mineral assemblages and mineral chemistry as functions of pressure and temperature, leading to key concepts such as metamorphic facies and thermobarometry;
(ii) interpret metamorphic textures to identify mineral reactions and metamorphic facies, using suites of thin sections to establish regional metamorphic geotherms and qualitative pressure–temperature–time (P–T–t) paths;
(iii) gain familiarity with both simple geothermobarometers and more complex, internally consistent datasets, such as Thermocalc and pseudosections, to enable quantitative determination of metamorphic conditions; and
(iv) analyze rock structures and textures to infer stress regimes during metamorphism and to draw conclusions about regional tectonic processes.

